[
    {
        "text": "Hey y’all, I wanted to follow up on the discussion of the shelter resource guide and how we can best distribute it to get it to folks who need to see it. I wanted to share some feedback I’ve received from the various providers who I have shared it with, as well as some statistics from Canva regarding the number of people who have viewed the graphic.\n\n• *From Miguel at the SF LGBT Center:* “this is absolutely amazing, thank you so much for sending and sharing! We’ve already passed this around internally and are planning to print it\/have it as a resource within the building. I’m checking in with our team to brainstorm some ideas we can send to you about where\/how to share this resource to get it in front of folks who will directly benefit from it, and will circle back. Again, we really appreciate this comprehensive resource, kudos to your team for pulling together!\n• *From Lane at LYRIC:* “Thank you so much for including us! The piece is great and the interactive map\/flowchart is an invaluable resource to have. I’ve shared the article with our organizational partners outside of HRS who often refer clients to us with the intent of them continuing to spread the word. Outside of what you’re already doing to disseminate the information, I’d recommend getting connected with SFHOT. Their team is going to be the fastest way to get this information directly to unhoused folks all across the city. I’d also recommend reaching out to organizations outside of HRS that interact with vulnerable populations. These providers assist unhoused people with meeting various needs (e.g., acquiring ID documents, getting connected to food resources, etc), but very few organizations have a grasp on how the HRS works. I’m sure the graphic would be helpful to have posted so clients and providers alike can easily access a roadmap for support.”\n• *From Hope at Compass:* “thank you for sharing this great article and working on this crucial effort. In this moment the only idea I have is to get this into Street Sheet and leave printed versions at the Family Access Points, as well as Family Resource Centers. LMK if you’d like a connect to FRCs. Was great to speak with you!\nI am in contact with HSH already and shared the graphic with them. I also shared the piece with Jennifer Friedenbach at HSH and know she has looked at the graphic at least once, because when I am on the doc I am able to see other people who are looking at it at that point. I am going to ask Hope to connect me with the FRCs.\n\nRegarding the canva stats, it is interesting that the view time on the graphic is so long. I checked and the total view time does not include the time I spent creating\/editing it. It appears that some people are looking at the piece more than once (unique views versus viewers). The link engagements show the number of links within the canva piece that others are clicking on. I could not figure out if the statistics include time people spend looking at the graphic in the article, or if it’s just when people specifically click on the canva link. Regardless, i thought this was interesting and i think making resource guides like this are a good way to give back to community, test out a different approach to a “public press style” article, and connect with potential new sources.",
